  <artifactId>s12-cqrs-read-model</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>s12-cqrs-read-model</name>
  <description>S12: a read model that no join could produce, and what maintaining it costs.</description>

  <!--
    Two services, because the projection's justification is cross-context.

    S20 already shows a list endpoint served straight from the write model, with paging done properly —
    and for a list of one context's own data that is the right answer, projection or not. What forces a
    projection here is that the list needs a product name, and products belong to another bounded
    context in another database. No SQL join reaches it. That, rather than a row count, is the
    threshold this sample exists to name.

    No third module, unlike S10 — and the difference is worth naming rather than assuming. There the
    property under test (one global transaction spanning two resources) genuinely cannot be observed one
    service at a time, so a harness booting both was the only honest instrument. Here the property is the
    wire record: the catalogue's test asserts the record it emits, the ordering service's test starts from
    a record on the topic and asserts what it does with it. Each side is tested against the contract, which
    is also what a foreign producer or consumer would exercise. S4 settled that pattern; S12 reuses it.
  -->
  <modules>
    <module>catalog-service</module>
    <module>ordering-service</module>
  </modules>
</project>
